---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleFeedback from "../../components/ArticleFeedback.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Why You Look Better in the Mirror Than in Photos"
    description="Why you may feel like you look better in the mirror than in photos, and why unfamiliarity can sometimes feel like something is wrong."
>
    <main class="article-page">
        <article>
            <header class="article-header">
                <p class="eyebrow">PHOTOS &amp; CAMERA CONFIDENCE</p>

                <h1>Why You Look Better in the Mirror Than in Photos</h1>

                <p class="article-intro">
                    The photo can feel wrong in ways that are hard to explain.
                </p>
            </header>

            <div class="article-content">
                <p>
                    There are photos of yourself that make you stop and think:
                </p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>That doesn't look like me.</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>
                    Which is strange, because you've just spent the whole day
                    looking at that face in the mirror.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The photo can feel wrong in ways that are hard to explain.
                </p>

                <p>Your smile looks different.</p>

                <p>One side of your face seems more noticeable.</p>

                <p>
                    Somehow, you look less like the person you see when you get
                    ready in the morning.
                </p>

                <p>
                    It can be tempting to conclude that the mirror shows the
                    real you and the camera shows something worse.
                </p>

                <p>It isn't quite that simple.</p>

                <h2>You're much more familiar with your reflection</h2>

                <p>You see your reflection all the time.</p>

                <p>Not just your face, either.</p>

                <p>You see yourself moving.</p>

                <p>Getting ready.</p>

                <p>Fixing your hair.</p>

                <p>Checking your clothes.</p>

                <p>Turning your head.</p>

                <p>Smiling at yourself.</p>

                <p>
                    Making some completely unimportant expression while thinking
                    about something else.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You have seen thousands of versions of yourself in a mirror
                    without really studying any of them.
                </p>

                <p>A photograph is different.</p>

                <p>It takes one moment and freezes it.</p>

                <p>Now you're looking at yourself as an image.</p>

                <p>
                    And once you start looking at an image, it's very easy to
                    notice things you normally wouldn't.
                </p>

                <p>The angle of your face.</p>

                <p>The position of your mouth.</p>

                <p>One eyebrow.</p>

                <p>A small expression you didn't know you were making.</p>

                <p>
                    You are not necessarily seeing a worse version of yourself.
                </p>

                <p>You're seeing a version you're less familiar with.</p>

                <h2>The mirror doesn't usually ask you to judge your face</h2>

                <p>This is another small difference.</p>

                <p>
                    When you look in the mirror, you usually have something else
                    to do.
                </p>

                <p>You're getting ready.</p>

                <p>Checking your outfit.</p>

                <p>Brushing your hair.</p>

                <p>Looking for something.</p>

                <p>You are not normally standing there thinking:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>Is this what I actually look like?</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>A photograph changes the situation.</p>

                <p>Now the activity is looking at yourself.</p>

                <p>And that can make you much more critical.</p>

                <p>You start comparing.</p>

                <p>You notice details.</p>

                <p>You zoom in.</p>

                <p>You take another photo.</p>

                <p>Compare that one.</p>

                <p>Then another.</p>

                <p>
                    At some point, you are no longer asking whether the photo
                    looks like you.
                </p>

                <p>You're inspecting your face.</p>

                <p>That's a very different experience.</p>

                <h2>A photo also removes movement</h2>

                <p>This part is easy to underestimate.</p>

                <p>
                    You don't normally experience someone's face as a still
                    image.
                </p>

                <p>You see it while they talk.</p>

                <p>While they smile.</p>

                <p>While they turn their head.</p>

                <p>While they react to something.</p>

                <p>The same is true when you see yourself in a mirror.</p>

                <p>Your reflection is moving.</p>

                <p>A photograph removes all of that.</p>

                <p>
                    It takes one expression out of a continuous stream of
                    expressions and asks you to look at it by itself.
                </p>

                <p>Sometimes the moment looks great.</p>

                <p>Sometimes it doesn't.</p>

                <p>But either way, it is only one moment.</p>

                <p>
                    It doesn't contain everything you looked like before it or
                    after it.
                </p>

                <h2>You may also be more used to one version of yourself</h2>

                <p>
                    There is something else going on when you compare mirrors
                    and photos.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The face you see in a mirror is familiar because you've seen
                    it repeatedly from that perspective.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A photograph may show you from a slightly different angle.
                </p>

                <p>
                    And because you aren't used to seeing yourself that way, the
                    difference can feel bigger than it actually is.
                </p>

                <p>You can look at a photo and think:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>My face looks strange.</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>But sometimes what feels strange is simply:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>I don't usually see myself like this.</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>Those are not the same thing.</p>

                <p>
                    Familiarity is very good at disguising itself as
                    correctness.
                </p>

                <h2>This is why taking more photos doesn't always help</h2>

                <p>
                    It seems logical that if you don't like a photo, you should
                    take another one.
                </p>

                <p>Then another.</p>

                <p>Eventually you'll get the good one.</p>

                <p>Sometimes you do.</p>

                <p>
                    But taking dozens of photos can also make the problem worse.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You become more familiar with the process of judging
                    yourself.
                </p>

                <p>You start noticing tiny differences between frames.</p>

                <p>This smile is better.</p>

                <p>That angle is worse.</p>

                <p>My face looks different here.</p>

                <p>Why does my nose look like that?</p>

                <p>You are no longer evaluating photographs as moments.</p>

                <p>You're comparing versions of your face.</p>

                <p>And there is almost always another detail to find.</p>

                <h2>
                    A photograph isn't supposed to look exactly like your
                    reflection
                </h2>

                <p>This may be the most useful reframe.</p>

                <p>
                    The goal isn't to find the photo that looks exactly like the
                    mirror.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The mirror and the camera are giving you different
                    experiences of the same person.
                </p>

                <p>
                    One shows you while you are moving through an ordinary
                    moment.
                </p>

                <p>The other freezes one particular frame.</p>

                <p>
                    Neither one is a complete description of what you look like.
                </p>

                <p>
                    So when a photograph feels unfamiliar, that doesn't
                    automatically mean something has gone wrong.
                </p>

                <p>
                    It may simply be showing you a version of yourself you
                    haven't spent much time looking at.
                </p>

                <h2>What feels unfamiliar can still look good</h2>

                <p>This is where things get a little confusing.</p>

                <p>
                    A photo can feel strange to you and still look perfectly
                    good to someone else.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You're comparing the image with a version of yourself you
                    know extremely well.
                </p>

                <p>Someone else doesn't have that reference point.</p>

                <p>They aren't thinking:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>That's not how my face normally looks.</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>They're just seeing you.</p>

                <p>
                    This doesn't mean you should ignore every photo you dislike.
                </p>

                <p>Some photos genuinely don't work.</p>

                <p>
                    The useful part is learning not to treat unfamiliarity as
                    automatic evidence that the photograph is bad.
                </p>

                <p>Those are two different judgments.</p>

                <h2>You may look better when you're not studying yourself</h2>

                <p>
                    There is a reason photos taken while you're doing something
                    can sometimes feel easier to like.
                </p>

                <p>You're not standing there examining your face.</p>

                <p>You're walking.</p>

                <p>Talking.</p>

                <p>Looking at something.</p>

                <p>Holding a coffee.</p>

                <p>Doing whatever you were already doing.</p>

                <p>Your attention is somewhere else.</p>

                <p>Your expression is changing naturally.</p>

                <p>The camera catches one part of that movement.</p>

                <p>You may still dislike some of the frames.</p>

                <p>
                    But you're less likely to spend the entire moment managing
                    how your face looks.
                </p>

                <p>
                    And that can make the photograph feel more like a person and
                    less like an inspection.
                </p>

                <h2>You don't have to learn to love every photo of yourself</h2>

                <p>That isn't really the goal.</p>

                <p>Some photographs will be unflattering.</p>

                <p>Some will catch strange expressions.</p>

                <p>Some will simply feel unfamiliar.</p>

                <p>That's normal.</p>

                <p>The useful shift is smaller.</p>

                <p>Instead of immediately thinking:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>I look bad in photos.</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>You can ask:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>What feels strange about this one?</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>Maybe it's the angle.</p>

                <p>Maybe it's the expression.</p>

                <p>Maybe you're comparing it with your reflection.</p>

                <p>Maybe you're simply not used to seeing yourself that way.</p>

                <p>
                    That question gives you more information than a blanket
                    judgment does.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Because sometimes the problem isn't that you look worse in
                    photos.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You're just much more familiar with the person you see in
                    the mirror.
                </p>

                <p>And familiar doesn't always mean more accurate.</p>

                <p>It just means familiar.</p>
            </div>

            <ArticleFeedback
                article="why-you-look-better-in-the-mirror-than-in-photos"
            />
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    .article-page {
